                <pb facs="0816" n="706" sameAs="frus:frus1931v03/pg706"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/3323: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-12-26T12:00:00+08:00">December 26,
                        1931—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received December 26—1:30 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>1129. Following from American Consul General at Harbin: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“December 23, 5 p.m.</p>
                        <list>
                            <label>1.</label>
                            <item>Last evening Secretary Chao, who had just returned from visiting
                                General Ma at Hailun, informed me that Ma had sufficient funds to
                                support his troops for 3 months; that he was still acting under
                                instructions of Nanking and that he would await the results of the
                                visit of the League of Nations commission.</item>
                            <label>2.</label>
                            <item>No evidence to the effect that Ma has yielded to Japanese demands
                                has been brought to the attention of this office, but judging by
                                Chao’s remarks he is anxious in regard to expected operations of
                                Japanese at Chinchow.[”]</item>
                        </list>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
